PART 3: CONNECTIVITY vs SCORE MULTIPLICITY
------------------------------------------------------------

  HYPOTHESIS: A fiber is connected iff its score sequence has
  "enough" equal or adjacent scores to enable score-preserving flips.

  Score-preserving flips need |score_i - score_j| ≤ 1.
  If all scores are distinct (transitive: 0,1,2,3,4), there are
  MANY adjacent pairs → connected fiber.
  If some scores are clustered (0,2,2,2,4), the gap between 0 and 2
  and between 2 and 4 means some arcs CAN'T be score-preservingly flipped.

  Let's check which arcs can be score-preservingly flipped:

       (0, 1, 2, 3, 4): 4/10 arcs flippable (within fiber)
       (0, 1, 3, 3, 3): 1/10 arcs flippable (within fiber)
       (0, 2, 2, 2, 4): 0/10 arcs flippable (within fiber)
       (0, 2, 2, 3, 3): 3/10 arcs flippable (within fiber)
       (1, 1, 1, 3, 4): 1/10 arcs flippable (within fiber)
       (1, 1, 2, 2, 4): 3/10 arcs flippable (within fiber)
       (1, 1, 2, 3, 3): 4/10 arcs flippable (within fiber)
       (1, 2, 2, 2, 3): 4/10 arcs flippable (within fiber)
       (2, 2, 2, 2, 2): 0/10 arcs flippable (within fiber)


